ty leaders are  vowing to end for-profit group homes and  foster care agencies after a scathing  investigation by global news and aptn  youth in care said they were neglected  mistreated and repeatedly physically  restrained by workers without cause  carolyn jarvis was part of the  investigative team that worked on this  project for a year and joins us with  more carolyn  nithu we received an outpouring of  response to our investigation from right  across the country people who say they  too were victims of a broken child  welfare system that left them scarred  and traumatized some experts suggest one  of the reasons why the system is failing  is because private for-profit operators  might be putting money ahead of the best  interests of kids the child welfare  system shouldn't be a profit a profit  making endeavor it just shouldn't the  leader of the ontario ndp vowing to  remove for-profit group home and foster  care operators from the system if  elected the fact that kids are not  getting enough food that they're being  restrained that they're not getting the  health care services they need while  some company reaps the profits off of  that completely unacceptable a global  news aptn investigation analyzed more  than 10 000 serious occurrence reports  from the child welfare system in ontario  from a one-year period and found an  alarmingly high number of incidents at  some foster care and group homes  including deaths injuries missing kids  and the use of restraints  and while private operators only make up  25 percent of beds in the system they  account for 55 percent of all serious  occurrence reports in group homes and  foster care homes including 83 percent  of all physical restraints the  for-profit sector should not exist  advocates for young people in care say  they've known for a long time how bad  things really are this has been a  long-standing issue that we knew about  on the ground i grew up in the child  welfare system myself and this is it's  about 